#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084066} :activity/id #uuid "654c7bcb-8774-5d52-8516-2b544db9deef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048891 :topic/id #uuid "c9c9a17a-1e46-5b3e-873e-a9145f5c883d" :topic/math-academy-id 698} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084516 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x}=\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhen the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nBefore we start, though, we note that $x=0$ *cannot* be a solution. This is because when $x=0$, we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side.\n\nNow, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =x\\cdot 3 \\\\ 10 & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can solve for $x$ using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x= \\dfrac{10}{3}$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x = \\dfrac{10}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "89ea4830-364e-5105-8df0-54cf9f7e1e36" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8779 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc5abf0d-f453-5c16-a078-3ade2b5464f0" :step/math-academy-id 170984 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082936 :question/id #uuid "5d89b84d-71cc-5c63-96f1-8abc8e2b2ca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14140"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "783c067b-9676-57f1-81a9-7724c18e1534" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492067}] :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations With Variables in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "a296ae16-d9ac-5336-88fb-84c518347b2a" :step/math-academy-id 170985 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084517 :tutorial/content "If there is a negative sign in a rational equation, we can still solve it using cross-multiplication. The only difference is that we have to move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction first.\n\nFor example, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = -\\dfrac 9 8 .\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the equation from the previous example, but this time, there is a negative sign on the right-hand side.\n\nWe deal with the negative sign by moving it onto the numerator of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed to solve the equation by cross-multiplication, as usual. (We also note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.) Cross-multiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} \\[5pt]\n\t\t3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot (-9) \\[5pt]\n\t\t24 &= -18x\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t24 &= -18x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{24}{-18} &= \\dfrac{-18x}{-18} \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{4}{3} &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = -\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d61fcf8b-f639-5c4c-8997-66813230bedf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8780 :tutorial/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs"} :step/id #uuid "ccd40655-e59d-59b1-925d-4907d84cca52" :step/math-academy-id 170988 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082937 :question/id #uuid "4a26be93-e244-596b-9341-383dbbbc3e88" :question/math-academy-id "e-14142"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20c12caf-9f3e-5dcb-a12c-5c368824ede9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492034}] :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs"} :step/id #uuid "5cdcec66-242d-5007-990f-d0802020baee" :step/math-academy-id 170989 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083194 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082938 :question/id #uuid "cbd8760f-af04-5196-90d6-0d4cfdfaec2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14145"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8f96a06-1ebb-5ed1-9e47-23525e2a756e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491997}] :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations With Binomials in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "1970d372-6794-5a8a-8180-36e7434e572b" :step/math-academy-id 170992}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Using Cross-Multiplication" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083407}]}
